76
00:04:29,510 --> 00:04:33,490
So we had-- so last time, we
talked about the theorem--

77
00:04:33,490 --> 00:04:35,030
we didn't prove it--

78
00:04:35,030 --> 00:04:43,120
that the reals is a
complete ordered field.

79
00:04:48,590 --> 00:04:52,580
And so, in particular,
it was an ordered set

80
00:04:52,580 --> 00:04:55,780
that was also a field.

81
00:04:55,780 --> 00:04:57,120
And it was complete.

82
00:04:57,120 --> 00:04:59,120
And so, again,
"complete" meant--

83
00:04:59,120 --> 00:05:06,340
so if you had a subset A
here, which was a subset of R,

84
00:05:06,340 --> 00:05:08,250
then A is bounded--

85
00:05:10,940 --> 00:05:29,660
is bounded from above
if there exists M in R

86
00:05:29,660 --> 00:05:43,510
such that for all a in A, a
here is less than or equal to M.

87
00:05:43,510 --> 00:05:46,220
And then, if that's
the case, then we

88
00:05:46,220 --> 00:06:08,920
say that an M here is said
to be an upper bound for A.

89
00:06:08,920 --> 00:06:21,380
Likewise, if A here
is a subset of R,

90
00:06:21,380 --> 00:06:39,660
then we say that A
is bounded from below

91
00:06:39,660 --> 00:06:59,620
if there exists little m in R
such that m here is less than a

92
00:06:59,620 --> 00:07:00,520
for all a.

93
00:07:05,930 --> 00:07:09,160
So this is upper and
lower bounds, OK?

94
00:07:09,160 --> 00:07:10,820
And several people ask--

95
00:07:10,820 --> 00:07:14,420
on Piazza, they ask--

96
00:07:14,420 --> 00:07:21,820
they were a little
unclear about,

97
00:07:21,820 --> 00:07:26,320
where does this M, capital M,
where does this have to be?

98
00:07:26,320 --> 00:07:28,380
In what set does it have to be?

99
00:07:28,380 --> 00:07:31,980
And likewise for
this little m, right?

100
00:07:31,980 --> 00:07:34,560
And so let me try to
give you some example.

101
00:07:34,560 --> 00:07:36,160
And I tried to do
that on Piazza.

102
00:07:36,160 --> 00:07:38,020
But let me just--

103
00:07:38,020 --> 00:07:40,080
because several people
were asking about it,

104
00:07:40,080 --> 00:07:42,060
let me just say again.

105
00:07:42,060 --> 00:07:46,320
So this capital M, when
you have it like this,

106
00:07:46,320 --> 00:07:51,250
this capital M does not need
to be in the set, right?

107
00:07:51,250 --> 00:07:52,270
A is bounded.

108
00:07:52,270 --> 00:07:53,180
So here's an example.

109
00:07:56,690 --> 00:08:01,690
If you're looking at the set,
0, 1-- so this is the interval.

110
00:08:01,690 --> 00:08:02,770
This is the interval.

111
00:08:02,770 --> 00:08:05,890
But it does not contain 0.

112
00:08:05,890 --> 00:08:07,530
And it does not contain 1.

113
00:08:07,530 --> 00:08:12,610
But it contains everything
in between 0 and 1.

114
00:08:12,610 --> 00:08:14,670
This one here is bounded.

115
00:08:14,670 --> 00:08:21,930
So if this has A, and we think
about A here as a subset of R,

116
00:08:21,930 --> 00:08:23,700
then A is bounded.

117
00:08:27,010 --> 00:08:31,530
And so one usually says
it's bounded if it's

118
00:08:31,530 --> 00:08:32,980
both from above and below.

119
00:08:43,250 --> 00:08:45,630
So if one just says
that it's bounded,

120
00:08:45,630 --> 00:08:49,930
then one means that it's
bounded from above and below.

121
00:08:49,930 --> 00:08:50,590
It's bounded.

122
00:08:53,470 --> 00:08:56,240
And so, because I
bounded from above--

123
00:09:01,170 --> 00:09:04,690
above, because you can just--
you have that if x here

124
00:09:04,690 --> 00:09:07,410
is in this interval,
then, of course,

125
00:09:07,410 --> 00:09:09,990
x is less than or
equal to 1, right?

126
00:09:09,990 --> 00:09:15,410
In fact, it's strictly
less than 1, right?

127
00:09:15,410 --> 00:09:17,970
But you see, this
set here, there

128
00:09:17,970 --> 00:09:24,370
is no element in this set that
is an upper bound for this set,

129
00:09:24,370 --> 00:09:25,250
right?

130
00:09:25,250 --> 00:09:27,990
The upper bound is
outside the set.

131
00:09:27,990 --> 00:09:30,970
But it is in all of
R. And that's fine.

132
00:09:30,970 --> 00:09:37,430
An upper bound will typically
just be in the larger set.

133
00:09:37,430 --> 00:09:43,570
And the same with lower bound.

134
00:09:43,570 --> 00:09:48,250
Now, this-- everything on this
board except this theorem--

135
00:09:48,250 --> 00:09:50,942
but this is in terms
of ordered sets.

136
00:09:50,942 --> 00:09:52,810
It has nothing to do--

137
00:09:52,810 --> 00:09:56,930
it's just much more general
than R. So if you take--

138
00:09:56,930 --> 00:10:06,050
if S here is an ordered set,
and A here is a subset--

139
00:10:09,178 --> 00:10:14,530
is a subset-- so
it's an ordered set.

140
00:10:14,530 --> 00:10:18,970
So it's a set with an ordering.

141
00:10:18,970 --> 00:10:25,050
And if A here is a subset
of S, then, again, we

142
00:10:25,050 --> 00:10:45,170
can say that A is bounded
from above if there exists M--

143
00:10:45,170 --> 00:10:50,170
again, M does not
have to be in A.

144
00:10:50,170 --> 00:10:52,390
But it can be just
in a larger set,

145
00:10:52,390 --> 00:11:06,390
so that-- such that for all a
in A, it's less or equal to M.

146
00:11:06,390 --> 00:11:12,810
So this is like the definition
in general, being bounded above,

147
00:11:12,810 --> 00:11:17,010
if you have a ordered set,
and you have a subset.

148
00:11:17,010 --> 00:11:19,170
And now, just to illustrate it.

149
00:11:19,170 --> 00:11:23,970
So typically, we're going to
think about where the set is

150
00:11:23,970 --> 00:11:26,170
R with the usual ordering.

151
00:11:26,170 --> 00:11:28,030
And you have a subset of it.

152
00:11:28,030 --> 00:11:32,410
And you talk about whether it's
bounded from above or below.

153
00:11:32,410 --> 00:11:34,170
But of course, you
could also do it

154
00:11:34,170 --> 00:11:37,390
in a more general
setting like this.

155
00:11:37,390 --> 00:11:43,600
And so here's an example, that
if you look at-- which maybe is

156
00:11:43,600 --> 00:11:45,360
counterintuitive--

157
00:11:45,360 --> 00:11:53,640
but if you take-- if S
here is the half line,

158
00:11:53,640 --> 00:11:57,120
so it's all the real numbers--

159
00:11:57,120 --> 00:12:02,140
it's between, say,
0 and infinity.

160
00:12:05,720 --> 00:12:12,120
And then you'll have A here.

161
00:12:12,120 --> 00:12:16,050
It's a subset, 0 to 1.

162
00:12:21,936 --> 00:12:27,500
Now, A here, and the ordering--
so this is, of course,

163
00:12:27,500 --> 00:12:30,480
a subset of R. But
you should forget

164
00:12:30,480 --> 00:12:33,840
about R. I mean, you
know the ordering from R.

165
00:12:33,840 --> 00:12:36,600
But forget about it,
because this is now what you

166
00:12:36,600 --> 00:12:40,600
think about the set S, right?

167
00:12:40,600 --> 00:12:45,840
And so, if you're looking A as
a subset of this, then A here--

168
00:12:45,840 --> 00:12:47,410
A is bounded from above.

169
00:12:55,920 --> 00:12:56,420
Why?

170
00:12:56,420 --> 00:13:02,040
This is just because,
for all A in A, you have

171
00:13:02,040 --> 00:13:05,280
that A here is
strictly less than 1.

172
00:13:05,280 --> 00:13:10,120
So 1 here is an upper
bound for A, right?

173
00:13:10,120 --> 00:13:18,040
But now, think about, does this
set here have a lower bound?

174
00:13:18,040 --> 00:13:23,740
Well, a lower bound have
to be inside this set, S,

175
00:13:23,740 --> 00:13:25,920
because we're thinking
about this here

176
00:13:25,920 --> 00:13:28,540
as a subset of this ordered set.

177
00:13:28,540 --> 00:13:32,000
We don't think about it as
a subset of the real line.

178
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:36,840
But if you think about it as
a subset of this set here,

179
00:13:36,840 --> 00:13:40,760
then there is no lower
bound, because 0 is not

180
00:13:40,760 --> 00:13:44,540
in this set, and no negative
numbers in this set.

181
00:13:44,540 --> 00:13:47,320
So this here-- and
this seems maybe

182
00:13:47,320 --> 00:13:49,040
a little bit counterintuitive.

183
00:13:49,040 --> 00:14:08,790
But A is not bounded from below
as a subset of S. Obviously,

184
00:14:08,790 --> 00:14:11,040
it is bounded from below if
you were thinking about it

185
00:14:11,040 --> 00:14:13,500
as a subset of a real line.

186
00:14:13,500 --> 00:14:16,040
But if you think about
it as a subset of S,

187
00:14:16,040 --> 00:14:19,600
it's not bounded from below.

188
00:14:19,600 --> 00:14:20,390
OK.

189
00:14:20,390 --> 00:14:31,760
So another person was asking
the infinite and minus infinity.

190
00:14:31,760 --> 00:14:35,830
Those are not real numbers, OK?

191
00:14:35,830 --> 00:14:36,565
Yeah?

192
00:14:36,565 --> 00:14:38,190
AUDIENCE: So on the
homework, should we

193
00:14:38,190 --> 00:14:41,593
consider all of the
