AUDIENCE: For question 3
on the homework [INAUDIBLE]

196
00:14:47,270 --> 00:14:52,470
they're all subsets of R.

197
00:14:52,470 --> 00:14:56,050
TOBIAS COLDING: If it's
not directly stated that--

198
00:14:56,050 --> 00:15:01,550
if it's not directly stated
that there's a set, S, and then

199
00:15:01,550 --> 00:15:04,830
it has to be a
subset of that set,

200
00:15:04,830 --> 00:15:09,270
then it would be assumed
that it's a-- yeah.

201
00:15:09,270 --> 00:15:13,870
Of course, you are
welcome to ask on Piazza.

202
00:15:13,870 --> 00:15:15,590
I don't have the homework here.

203
00:15:15,590 --> 00:15:17,670
But I would say
that you're safe--

204
00:15:17,670 --> 00:15:22,510
if it's not stated directly
what the larger set is, then

205
00:15:22,510 --> 00:15:24,350
you can assume it's
R, because we're going

206
00:15:24,350 --> 00:15:25,890
to concentrate mostly on R.

207
00:15:25,890 --> 00:15:29,350
This is another
distinction between Rudin

208
00:15:29,350 --> 00:15:32,090
and this book that
is the main textbook.

209
00:15:32,090 --> 00:15:37,590
Rudin is more abstract than
the main textbook we are using.

210
00:15:37,590 --> 00:15:42,890
In particular, the
main textbook here,

211
00:15:42,890 --> 00:15:44,890
and I'll do the
same in my lectures,

212
00:15:44,890 --> 00:15:47,910
is that a lot of things
are motivated from R.

213
00:15:47,910 --> 00:15:50,550
And then you
abstract afterwards,

214
00:15:50,550 --> 00:15:53,990
so whereas Rudin starts
with the abstraction,

215
00:15:53,990 --> 00:15:58,390
and then it specializes to
R. And of course, there's

216
00:15:58,390 --> 00:16:01,880
an argument for maybe starting
with the familiar and then

217
00:16:01,880 --> 00:16:03,880
abstract, instead of doing
the other way around.

218
00:16:10,510 --> 00:16:13,830
And so for a-- whether
it's just an ordered set--

219
00:16:13,830 --> 00:16:19,250
I mean, just in general, so
remember that completeness--

220
00:16:23,950 --> 00:16:28,910
so completeness-- so this is
when you have an ordered set.

221
00:16:28,910 --> 00:16:33,110
So if you have an
ordered set, and A here

222
00:16:33,110 --> 00:16:40,400
is a subset of S that
is bounded from above--

223
00:16:47,230 --> 00:16:55,530
from above-- then, it is
complete if S is complete.

224
00:16:55,530 --> 00:17:03,630
So if S is complete,
then any subset

225
00:17:03,630 --> 00:17:11,829
that is bounded from above
has a least upper bound.

226
00:17:11,829 --> 00:17:18,840
Then A has a least upper bound.

227
00:17:24,069 --> 00:17:31,050
And that least upper bound is
denoted by the sup of A, right?

228
00:17:31,050 --> 00:17:36,180
So this thing here,
it has the property

229
00:17:36,180 --> 00:17:48,340
that a is less than or equal
to M for all a in A. And also,

230
00:17:48,340 --> 00:17:54,430
if M1 is another upper bound--

231
00:17:57,060 --> 00:18:03,820
upper bound for A--

232
00:18:03,820 --> 00:18:08,560
then M is less than
or equal to M1.

233
00:18:11,300 --> 00:18:15,020
So that's a least upper bound.

234
00:18:15,020 --> 00:18:17,820
And the same thing is--

235
00:18:17,820 --> 00:18:22,860
so, similarly, when we
have an ordered set,

236
00:18:22,860 --> 00:18:26,770
and we have a set, if it's
complete, and if we have--

237
00:18:33,140 --> 00:18:56,460
if A here is bounded
from below, then inf of A

238
00:18:56,460 --> 00:19:06,460
is the least upper-- is
the largest, the greatest,

239
00:19:06,460 --> 00:19:14,400
lower bound for A.

240
00:19:14,400 --> 00:19:19,020
So this, again, means that
this number here, little m--

241
00:19:19,020 --> 00:19:28,620
so m here is less or
equal to a for all a in A.

242
00:19:28,620 --> 00:19:42,820
And if m1 here is
another lower bound,

243
00:19:42,820 --> 00:19:48,940
then m1 here is
less or equal to m.

244
00:19:48,940 --> 00:19:51,780
So this is a symmetric property.

245
00:19:51,780 --> 00:19:56,860
And so, again, R, the
reals, is complete.

246
00:19:56,860 --> 00:20:01,440
So whenever you take a
bounded subset of the real--

247
00:20:01,440 --> 00:20:04,180
so if it's bounded, that means
that it's bounded from above

248
00:20:04,180 --> 00:20:05,660
and below--

249
00:20:05,660 --> 00:20:09,880
then it has a least upper bound
and a greatest lower bound.

250
00:20:09,880 --> 00:20:15,220
And those are denoted
by sup and inf.

251
00:20:15,220 --> 00:20:19,620
And in general,
sup and inf are not

252
00:20:19,620 --> 00:20:24,660
in the set itself, in the
set A. I mean, they could be.

253
00:20:24,660 --> 00:20:27,627
But in general,
they wouldn't be.

254
00:20:27,627 --> 00:20:29,710
AUDIENCE: The example that
we talked about earlier

255
00:20:29,710 --> 00:20:32,860
[INAUDIBLE] open set
[INAUDIBLE] but that

256
00:20:32,860 --> 00:20:34,610
is not complete [INAUDIBLE].

257
00:20:34,610 --> 00:20:36,170
TOBIAS COLDING: That's right.

258
00:20:36,170 --> 00:20:38,430
So no.

259
00:20:38,430 --> 00:20:42,666
So if you're looking
at this set here,

260
00:20:42,666 --> 00:20:56,490
so suppose that this
here is the set S.

261
00:20:56,490 --> 00:21:01,610
And if you take A
here is a subset of S,

262
00:21:01,610 --> 00:21:06,250
and A here is bounded
from-- let's say that A here

263
00:21:06,250 --> 00:21:07,580
is bounded from below.

264
00:21:16,010 --> 00:21:20,930
If it's bounded from below,
then it couldn't be--

265
00:21:20,930 --> 00:21:26,530
this set here, this here
is not bounded from below.

266
00:21:26,530 --> 00:21:29,770
So it means that if
it's bounded from below,

267
00:21:29,770 --> 00:21:35,970
then A here could not have
numbers that was approaching 0.

268
00:21:35,970 --> 00:21:39,930
And so actually, if it
is bounded from below,

269
00:21:39,930 --> 00:21:42,890
if it's a subset of this,
and it's bounded from below,

270
00:21:42,890 --> 00:21:45,850
then actually, it would
have a least lower bound--

271
00:21:45,850 --> 00:21:47,590
a greatest lower bound, right?

272
00:21:47,590 --> 00:21:49,370
It should mean--

273
00:21:49,370 --> 00:21:51,130
Yeah.

274
00:21:51,130 --> 00:21:52,190
So it is--

275
00:21:52,190 --> 00:21:53,970
I mean, I'm not going
to prove anything

276
00:21:53,970 --> 00:21:55,810
is complete or not complete.

277
00:21:55,810 --> 00:22:07,330
But it's pretty convincing
that it is actually complete,

278
00:22:07,330 --> 00:22:08,010
in this sense.

279
00:22:08,010 --> 00:22:10,150
There's another sense
that is not complete.

280
00:22:10,150 --> 00:22:12,610
But anyway, in this
sense, it is complete.

281
00:22:12,610 --> 00:22:13,130
Yeah.

282
00:22:13,130 --> 00:22:15,730
No, it's a little bit--

283
00:22:15,730 --> 00:22:17,250
it can be confusing.

284
00:22:17,250 --> 00:22:19,730
Later on, we'll talk about
maybe some other notion

285
00:22:19,730 --> 00:22:20,670
of completeness.

286
00:22:20,670 --> 00:22:21,910
And then it's not complete.

287
00:22:21,910 --> 00:22:27,090
But in this notion,
it is complete.

288
00:22:27,090 --> 00:22:33,090
OK, so the next thing
I want to get to

289
00:22:33,090 --> 00:22:35,910
is this with how to write
a mathematical proof.

290
00:22:39,138 --> 00:22:44,570
And again, that's a
super important thing

291
00:22:44,570 --> 00:22:48,630
to learn in this class.

292
00:22:48,630 --> 00:22:52,850
And it's not just a--

293
00:22:52,850 --> 00:22:59,610
it's not just a formal thing.

294
00:22:59,610 --> 00:23:01,550
I mean, it's-- yeah.

295
00:23:01,550 --> 00:23:03,690
It may seem like a formal thing.

296
00:23:03,690 --> 00:23:07,210
Why bother with that?

297
00:23:07,210 --> 00:23:09,970
But it's really, really useful.

298
00:23:09,970 --> 00:23:16,850
And the more complicated things
become, the more useful it is.

299
00:23:16,850 --> 00:23:21,290
And so I want to
illustrate this with how

300
00:23:21,290 --> 00:23:23,200
to write a mathematical proof.

301
00:23:23,200 --> 00:23:25,420
So I'm going to
illustrate this--

302
00:23:25,420 --> 00:23:38,420
so we will illustrate
it by proving again--

303
00:23:41,110 --> 00:23:58,840
by writing a formal proof
of why square root of 2

304
00:23:58,840 --> 00:23:59,750
is a real number.

305
00:24:02,320 --> 00:24:04,880
So I want to write--

306
00:24:04,880 --> 00:24:09,440
so last time, we talked
about that square root of 2

307
00:24:09,440 --> 00:24:12,120
was a real number.

308
00:24:12,120 --> 00:24:14,840
But when we proved
it, it was more

309
00:24:14,840 --> 00:24:18,360
like we talked about the idea.

310
00:24:18,360 --> 00:24:21,500
And so then the
question is-- so if you

311
00:24:21,500 --> 00:24:24,440
have-- imagine you have
a piece of scrap paper.

312
00:24:24,440 --> 00:24:27,000
What we did last time
was what you would

313
00:24:27,000 --> 00:24:28,500
be writing on the scrap paper.

314
00:24:28,500 --> 00:24:33,360
So I want to talk about, if you
were handing it in or showing it

315
00:24:33,360 --> 00:24:37,920
to somebody, then
you wouldn't do that.

316
00:24:37,920 --> 00:24:40,160
You would do what we do now.

317
00:24:40,160 --> 00:24:44,280
But it doesn't necessarily make
for a great-- a formal proof

318
00:24:44,280 --> 00:24:47,200
doesn't necessarily make
for a great lecture,

319
00:24:47,200 --> 00:24:48,810
because it's kind of formal.

320
00:24:52,424 --> 00:24:58,120
So let me try to explain
how you should write this.

321
00:24:58,120 --> 00:25:02,040
And again, this is a
very simple example.

322
00:25:02,040 --> 00:25:07,420
But hopefully, it will give
you an idea of what to do.

323
00:25:07,420 --> 00:25:11,320
And again, this is a really
important part of this class

324
00:25:11,320 --> 00:25:18,560
that you're bound to use in
many other classes later on.

325
00:25:18,560 --> 00:25:22,600
OK, so let's first talk about
what was square root of 2.

326
00:25:22,600 --> 00:25:24,960
So square root of 2--

327
00:25:24,960 --> 00:25:27,240
this here was a number.

328
00:25:27,240 --> 00:25:32,720
And it was given by that it has
a property-- that it's positive,

329
00:25:32,720 --> 00:25:37,140
and when you square
it, then you get 2.

330
00:25:37,140 --> 00:25:39,120
That was what square
root of 2 was.

331
00:25:41,840 --> 00:25:51,480
Now, so we again-- so we want to
prove-- so this is the theorem.

332
00:25:51,480 --> 00:26:00,640
So theorem-- square root
of 2 is a real number.

333
00:26:00,640 --> 00:26:03,680
So I know-- maybe I should
write it more formally.

334
00:26:03,680 --> 00:26:14,215
There exists alpha
greater than 0--

335
00:26:14,215 --> 00:26:22,950
this is alpha in R with alpha
greater than 0 and alpha

336
00:26:22,950 --> 00:26:24,730
squared is equal to 2.

337
00:26:29,350 --> 00:26:41,590
Now, we look at the set A.
The set A is the numbers--

338
00:26:41,590 --> 00:26:51,830
it's the x, such
that x is positive,

339
00:26:51,830 --> 00:27:00,645
and x squared is less
than or equal to 2.

340
00:27:00,645 --> 00:27:03,355
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] see
what x is, like where--

341
00:27:03,355 --> 00:27:04,230
TOBIAS COLDING: Yeah.

342
00:27:04,230 --> 00:27:10,430
So x here-- so now I will
specialize to the real numbers.

343
00:27:10,430 --> 00:27:12,250
So that was why I didn't
actually write it.

344
00:27:12,250 --> 00:27:13,950
But you're right.

345
00:27:13,950 --> 00:27:15,520
It certainly can't hurt.

346
00:27:15,520 --> 00:27:17,590
OK, so that's the theorem.

347
00:27:17,590 --> 00:27:19,720
And so now we try to
write a formal proof.

348
00:27:26,030 --> 00:27:43,150
Observe first that
A here is nonempty,

349
00:27:43,150 --> 00:27:47,670
since 1 is in A, right?

350
00:27:47,670 --> 00:27:49,470
1 is positive.

351
00:27:49,470 --> 00:27:52,370
And when you square
1, you get 1.

352
00:27:52,370 --> 00:27:54,670
So this is definitely
less than or equal to 2.

353
00:27:54,670 --> 00:27:56,480
So A here is nonempty.

354
00:27:59,910 --> 00:28:14,870
Next, observe that
A here is bounded--

355
00:28:14,870 --> 00:28:15,860
is bounded from above.

356
00:28:21,870 --> 00:28:25,350
In general, by the way,
when you write a proof,

357
00:28:25,350 --> 00:28:28,950
then you really only try
to establish the properties

358
00:28:28,950 --> 00:28:31,810
that you need, because
otherwise, it's confusing.

359
00:28:31,810 --> 00:28:33,950
If you establish all
kinds of other properties

360
00:28:33,950 --> 00:28:37,390
that is not needed, that
just makes it confusing.

361
00:28:37,390 --> 00:28:39,180
So it's bounded from above.

362
00:28:41,870 --> 00:28:43,010
And this is just because--

363
00:28:48,590 --> 00:29:05,662
since 2 is an upper bound for A.

364
00:29:05,662 --> 00:29:08,070
Now, the next thing
is-- so now we have

365
00:29:08,070 --> 00:29:10,730
a non-empty bounded subset.

366
00:29:10,730 --> 00:29:18,700
So we set-- so set alpha
equal to the sup of a.

367
00:29:18,700 --> 00:29:21,020
And we want to prove--

368
00:29:21,020 --> 00:29:31,420
want to show that alpha is
positive, and alpha squared

369
00:29:31,420 --> 00:29:32,480
is equal to 2.

370
00:29:39,380 --> 00:29:49,960
Well-- all right.

371
00:29:49,960 --> 00:29:51,840
By definition, the
way we defined--

372
00:29:55,100 --> 00:30:01,020
the way we defined A, all the
elements in A were positive.

373
00:30:01,020 --> 00:30:12,090
So by definition,
0 is a lower bound.

374
00:30:17,060 --> 00:30:22,100
So you for sure have that 0
here is less or equal to--

375
00:30:22,100 --> 00:30:26,100
well, it's actually-- I mean,
even 1 is a lower bound.

376
00:30:26,100 --> 00:30:31,300
So you can also just say that
since 1 is a lower bound--

377
00:30:31,300 --> 00:30:37,340
since 1 here is in A, you
also have that this alpha here

378
00:30:37,340 --> 00:30:41,940
must be bigger or
equal to 1, because it

379
00:30:41,940 --> 00:30:46,940
was supposed to be an upper
bound for A, so in particular.

380
00:30:46,940 --> 00:30:55,220
Now-- and so we have that
alpha is less than 1.

381
00:30:55,220 --> 00:30:57,440
And it's the least upper bound.

382
00:30:57,440 --> 00:30:59,040
And 2 was an upper bound.

383
00:30:59,040 --> 00:31:02,980
So you have this, since--

384
00:31:02,980 --> 00:31:07,820
maybe I should write
it with this thing.

385
00:31:07,820 --> 00:31:14,780
Since 1 is in A, we
have that 1 here,

386
00:31:14,780 --> 00:31:15,950
less than or equal to alpha.

387
00:31:19,700 --> 00:31:35,900
And since alpha is
the least upper bound,

388
00:31:35,900 --> 00:31:49,140
and 2 is an upper bound,
we have that alpha

389
00:31:49,140 --> 00:31:50,480
is less than or equal to 2.

390
00:31:53,020 --> 00:31:55,580
Now, remember, we were
going to do it exactly

391
00:31:55,580 --> 00:31:56,840
like we did last time.

392
00:31:56,840 --> 00:32:01,860
But we're just going to write it
down in a proper way as a proof.

393
00:32:01,860 --> 00:32:04,820
So remember that last time,
we then divided the proof

394
00:32:04,820 --> 00:32:08,300
into two steps.

395
00:32:08,300 --> 00:32:10,620
The first step was--

396
00:32:10,620 --> 00:32:15,900
so part 1-- so now, so all
we need to prove-- maybe

397
00:32:15,900 --> 00:32:17,140
I'll just say that.

398
00:32:17,140 --> 00:32:26,980
All we need to
prove, to show, is

399
00:32:26,980 --> 00:32:31,020
that alpha squared
is equal to 2,

400
00:32:31,020 --> 00:32:33,100
because we already have
it as positive now,

401
00:32:33,100 --> 00:32:36,016
because it's bigger than 1.

402
00:32:36,016 --> 00:32:40,260
And so there was two parts
to this, step 1 and step 2,

403
00:32:40,260 --> 00:32:43,140
or part 1 and part 2.

404
00:32:43,140 --> 00:32:46,180
The first part
was-- so part 1, we

405
00:32:46,180 --> 00:33:00,930
will show that alpha squared
is less than or equal to 2.

406
00:33:07,106 --> 00:33:09,510
And so we prove this--

407
00:33:09,510 --> 00:33:13,320
so assume not.

408
00:33:19,250 --> 00:33:20,770
We will show that that leads--

409
00:33:29,955 --> 00:33:50,290
that this leads to a
contradiction to that alpha

410
00:33:50,290 --> 00:34:03,270
is the least upper bound for A.

411
00:34:03,270 --> 00:34:07,310
I'm writing everything out
here, just to illustrate it.

412
00:34:07,310 --> 00:34:11,170
As you get more and
more familiar with this,

413
00:34:11,170 --> 00:34:13,370
then the structure is the same.

414
00:34:13,370 --> 00:34:16,949
But some of these steps will
seem almost too trivial.

415
00:34:16,949 --> 00:34:18,610
And that's probably fine.

416
00:34:18,610 --> 00:34:21,850
But of course, it's
always good to err on more

417
00:34:21,850 --> 00:34:23,759
details than fewer details.

418
00:34:27,596 --> 00:34:29,929
So this is what we want to do.

419
00:34:29,929 --> 00:34:35,730
So we assume not.

420
00:34:35,730 --> 00:34:44,050
So assume that alpha squared
is strictly bigger than 2.

421
00:34:47,330 --> 00:34:50,409
Then-- and this is
where it deviates more

422
00:34:50,409 --> 00:34:54,090
from how we did it last time.

423
00:34:54,090 --> 00:34:56,090
And that's why what
we did last time

424
00:34:56,090 --> 00:35:02,970
is really what you want to
have on the scrap paper.

425
00:35:02,970 --> 00:35:06,770
What we did last time
was like the idea.

426
00:35:06,770 --> 00:35:10,090
And then, once you
see the idea, then

427
00:35:10,090 --> 00:35:16,450
you try to write it as a formal
proof, like what we do now.

428
00:35:16,450 --> 00:35:21,530
And so, so assume not.

429
00:35:21,530 --> 00:35:25,410
Set h equal to--

430
00:35:25,410 --> 00:35:30,930
so I'm going to set h equal
to alpha squared minus 2

431
00:35:30,930 --> 00:35:32,760
over 4 alpha.

432
00:35:35,530 --> 00:35:39,010
Obviously, if this was the
first time you would do it,

433
00:35:39,010 --> 00:35:41,910
you wouldn't just come up with
this sort of thing, right?

434
00:35:41,910 --> 00:35:45,090
This is coming from the
scrap paper that you had.

435
00:35:45,090 --> 00:35:47,610
So you're setting
h equal to that.

436
00:35:47,610 --> 00:35:48,740
And then you observe--

437
00:35:51,970 --> 00:36:04,000
you observe that since alpha
here is bigger than 1, and alpha

438
00:36:04,000 --> 00:36:09,920
squared is bigger
than 2, since that,

439
00:36:09,920 --> 00:36:13,600
then the nominator
here is positive.

440
00:36:13,600 --> 00:36:15,800
And the denominator is positive.

441
00:36:15,800 --> 00:36:17,960
So h here is positive.

442
00:36:21,280 --> 00:36:23,420
You also have that--

443
00:36:23,420 --> 00:36:25,670
you also have that h is--

444
00:36:30,460 --> 00:36:30,960
right.

445
00:36:30,960 --> 00:36:36,680
You also have
automatically that h is--

446
00:36:36,680 --> 00:36:37,780
maybe we don't need that.

447
00:36:37,780 --> 00:36:38,940
Let's see if we need that.

448
00:36:38,940 --> 00:36:42,120
So OK.

449
00:36:42,120 --> 00:36:55,520
So now you set alpha 1
equal to alpha minus h.

450
00:36:55,520 --> 00:37:10,300
And what I want to prove is-- so
we want to show that alpha here,

451
00:37:10,300 --> 00:37:21,520
alpha 1, is an
upper bound for A.

452
00:37:21,520 --> 00:37:25,160
So I want to prove that this
is an upper bound for A.

453
00:37:25,160 --> 00:37:28,840
The first thing I want to do--
if it's an upper bound, it does

454
00:37:28,840 --> 00:37:30,620
need to be positive.

455
00:37:30,620 --> 00:37:31,630
So let's check that.

456
00:37:40,120 --> 00:37:46,360
So since we already have
that h was positive--

457
00:37:46,360 --> 00:37:51,440
so this here was h was
positive, alpha squared minus 2

458
00:37:51,440 --> 00:37:53,800
over 4 alpha.

459
00:37:53,800 --> 00:37:57,360
This thing here is
less or equal to--

460
00:37:57,360 --> 00:37:59,020
I can even forget about the 2.

461
00:37:59,020 --> 00:38:02,760
So I can write it as alpha
squared over 4 alpha.

462
00:38:02,760 --> 00:38:04,360
And so this here is--

463
00:38:04,360 --> 00:38:06,400
I can just divide by alpha.

464
00:38:06,400 --> 00:38:09,080
So I get alpha over 4.

465
00:38:09,080 --> 00:38:14,200
So I have-- since we have that h
here is less than alpha over 4,

466
00:38:14,200 --> 00:38:22,020
then alpha 1 is bigger than--

467
00:38:22,020 --> 00:38:30,208
is bigger or equal
to 3/4 of alpha,

468
00:38:30,208 --> 00:38:34,400
because I'm subtracting
at most alpha over 4.

469
00:38:34,400 --> 00:38:37,840
And so there's still 3/4
of alpha left, right?

470
00:38:37,840 --> 00:38:40,920
So I have this-- in
particular, this thing here.

471
00:38:40,920 --> 00:38:43,720
It's actually bigger
than 3 over 4.

472
00:38:43,720 --> 00:38:45,380
But I just need
it to be positive.

473
00:38:49,640 --> 00:38:51,240
So that's positive.

474
00:38:51,240 --> 00:38:58,670
So alpha over--
alpha 1 is positive.

475
00:38:58,670 --> 00:39:04,990
Now I'm looking at
alpha 1 squared, right?

476
00:39:04,990 --> 00:39:13,260
And so alpha 1 squared is
equal to alpha minus h squared.

477
00:39:15,950 --> 00:39:21,950
So this here is equal
to alpha squared plus h

478
00:39:21,950 --> 00:39:24,930
squared minus 2 alpha h.

479
00:39:28,940 --> 00:39:36,830
And this thing here is less
or equal to alpha squared.

480
00:39:36,830 --> 00:39:40,470
Here, h here is--

481
00:39:40,470 --> 00:39:47,950
h is-- so now I'd like to say
that h is less or equal to 1.

482
00:39:47,950 --> 00:39:51,030
Actually, I'm going to use
that it's less than 1/2.

483
00:39:51,030 --> 00:39:52,890
And why is that the case?

484
00:39:52,890 --> 00:39:56,330
It's actually, alpha itself--

485
00:39:56,330 --> 00:39:59,430
we already know that
alpha was less than 2.

486
00:39:59,430 --> 00:40:06,310
So you have that this thing here
is less or equal to 2 over 4.

487
00:40:06,310 --> 00:40:09,290
So this is equal to 1/2, right?

488
00:40:14,830 --> 00:40:20,230
So now I have that--

489
00:40:20,230 --> 00:40:27,750
now I have that this thing
here is less than plus--

490
00:40:30,510 --> 00:40:36,250
here, I just use that it's
less than this thing here.

491
00:40:36,250 --> 00:40:37,190
This here, I just--

492
00:40:37,190 --> 00:40:40,850
I think it's fine
to just use h here.

493
00:40:40,850 --> 00:40:42,130
I could use h over 2.

494
00:40:42,130 --> 00:40:43,450
