And so this is this alpha 2.

746
00:58:04,830 --> 00:58:08,170
So I'm defining--
so I'm setting--

747
00:58:08,170 --> 00:58:16,890
set this and alpha 2
equal to alpha plus h.

748
00:58:16,890 --> 00:58:18,270
This here was just
to illustrate.

749
00:58:18,270 --> 00:58:21,150
You don't necessarily
have to include that.

750
00:58:23,950 --> 00:58:26,170
And I want to show--

751
00:58:26,170 --> 00:58:27,350
we want to show--

752
00:58:30,170 --> 00:58:33,210
I think it's a good idea to
write what you want to show.

753
00:58:33,210 --> 00:58:36,890
In particular, if I really
had written that before,

754
00:58:36,890 --> 00:58:38,490
then I wouldn't have
been in trouble,

755
00:58:38,490 --> 00:58:42,050
because I was trying to
prove something else.

756
00:58:42,050 --> 00:58:48,570
So you want to prove
that alpha squared here

757
00:58:48,570 --> 00:58:52,410
is also less than 2.

758
00:58:52,410 --> 00:59:03,190
So this is-- so we already have
that alpha here is positive.

759
00:59:10,530 --> 00:59:13,170
It's bigger than 1.

760
00:59:13,170 --> 00:59:17,850
So this number here, this
here, we already know,

761
00:59:17,850 --> 00:59:19,090
is bigger than alpha.

762
00:59:23,410 --> 00:59:26,450
And so, if I can prove that--

763
00:59:26,450 --> 00:59:27,890
so this here is--

764
00:59:27,890 --> 00:59:29,510
so it's bigger than alpha.

765
00:59:29,510 --> 00:59:32,690
If I can prove that the
square is less than 2,

766
00:59:32,690 --> 00:59:34,650
then alpha here--

767
00:59:34,650 --> 00:59:37,630
alpha here, sorry-- would
not be an upper bound.

768
00:59:37,630 --> 00:59:44,030
There's another element in
the set A that is bigger.

769
00:59:44,030 --> 00:59:45,170
So I want to prove that.

770
00:59:45,170 --> 00:59:49,910
So now I'm looking
at alpha 2 squared.

771
00:59:49,910 --> 00:59:53,750
So that is alpha plus h, right?

772
00:59:53,750 --> 00:59:55,930
That's how I define alpha 2.

773
00:59:55,930 --> 00:59:57,110
And I square it.

774
01:00:00,090 --> 01:00:02,290
And if I do that,
then I get alpha

775
01:00:02,290 --> 01:00:12,660
squared plus h squared
plus 2 h alpha.

776
01:00:16,180 --> 01:00:20,260
And I now-- so now I
want an upper bound.

777
01:00:20,260 --> 01:00:26,480
And so we already have that
alpha is this one here.

778
01:00:26,480 --> 01:00:31,560
h is-- you see that h
is, in this case here--

779
01:00:31,560 --> 01:00:38,600
so h is equal to 2 minus
alpha squared over 4 alpha.

780
01:00:38,600 --> 01:00:42,240
Alpha is bigger or equal to 1.

781
01:00:42,240 --> 01:00:45,640
This here, the denominator,
is bounded by 2.

782
01:00:45,640 --> 01:00:47,180
So again, it's 2 over 4.

783
01:00:47,180 --> 01:00:50,920
So this is bounded by 1/2.

784
01:00:50,920 --> 01:00:54,340
So h is positive.

785
01:00:54,340 --> 01:01:01,000
And we have that h is positive
and h is less than 1/2, right?

786
01:01:01,000 --> 01:01:06,040
And so now, this
here, I can bound.

787
01:01:06,040 --> 01:01:10,520
I can bound trivially by just
using that h is bounded by 1.

788
01:01:10,520 --> 01:01:15,920
So I get this plus h plus 2.

789
01:01:15,920 --> 01:01:22,920
And then h times alpha,
again, or 2h alpha, this is--

790
01:01:22,920 --> 01:01:27,720
when we multiply this by
2 alpha, then the alpha

791
01:01:27,720 --> 01:01:28,840
cancel out.

792
01:01:28,840 --> 01:01:34,080
And the 2 cancel
with one of these--

793
01:01:34,080 --> 01:01:35,360
2 and this 4.

794
01:01:35,360 --> 01:01:39,140
So you're getting 2 minus
alpha squared over 2.

795
01:01:43,010 --> 01:01:49,280
And so now I get that this
thing here is bounded by--

796
01:01:49,280 --> 01:01:56,600
so h here, h is also at--

797
01:01:56,600 --> 01:02:01,920
a very rough bound for
h is using-- sorry--

798
01:02:01,920 --> 01:02:04,760
so using-- so I
have that, and now

799
01:02:04,760 --> 01:02:16,920
using that h here is equal to
alpha 2 minus alpha squared

800
01:02:16,920 --> 01:02:24,440
over 2 over 4 alpha,
which is less than 2

801
01:02:24,440 --> 01:02:31,520
minus alpha squared
over 2, since alpha here

802
01:02:31,520 --> 01:02:34,180
is bigger than 1, right?

803
01:02:34,180 --> 01:02:35,880
So I have that.

804
01:02:35,880 --> 01:02:46,120
So using that, we have
that alpha 2 squared

805
01:02:46,120 --> 01:02:49,120
is less or equal to-- let
me just write that line.

806
01:02:49,120 --> 01:02:50,880
I'm just now filling in.

807
01:02:50,880 --> 01:02:53,080
Alpha squared, the H here--

808
01:02:53,080 --> 01:02:55,760
I'm using this inequality here--

809
01:02:55,760 --> 01:02:59,120
2 minus alpha squared over 2.

810
01:02:59,120 --> 01:03:03,160
And then I have the
same thing again.

811
01:03:03,160 --> 01:03:07,560
And so you see that this
here, it's this here times 2.

812
01:03:07,560 --> 01:03:15,400
So this is just alpha squared
minus 2 plus 2 minus alpha

813
01:03:15,400 --> 01:03:15,900
squared.

814
01:03:19,230 --> 01:03:24,030
And this inequality here,
one of these inequalities, I

815
01:03:24,030 --> 01:03:26,690
should have replaced
by a strict inequality.

816
01:03:26,690 --> 01:03:30,370
This is because h is positive
and is strictly less than 1.

817
01:03:30,370 --> 01:03:32,630
So this here is actually
a strict inequality.

818
01:03:32,630 --> 01:03:34,890
So this here is a
strict inequality.

819
01:03:37,650 --> 01:03:42,750
But now you see that this thing
here, the alpha cancel out.

820
01:03:42,750 --> 01:03:44,750
And you're getting 2.

821
01:03:44,750 --> 01:03:50,350
So you have that alpha 2
squared is strictly less than 2.

822
01:03:50,350 --> 01:03:52,630
That's what we wanted to prove.

823
01:03:52,630 --> 01:04:07,150
And this gives that alpha 2
is also in A. And yet, alpha 2

824
01:04:07,150 --> 01:04:10,750
was bigger than alpha.

825
01:04:10,750 --> 01:04:13,130
And this is the
desired contradiction.

826
01:04:25,096 --> 01:04:28,530
So again, we already
did this last time.

827
01:04:28,530 --> 01:04:32,230
But the point here
is that, how do you

828
01:04:32,230 --> 01:04:34,070
write down a formal proof?

829
01:04:34,070 --> 01:04:36,030
And a formal proof,
in this case,

830
01:04:36,030 --> 01:04:38,850
is that you try-- very
clearly, in this case,

831
01:04:38,850 --> 01:04:41,390
it was divided into two steps.

832
01:04:41,390 --> 01:04:45,330
So you try to divide
it into the two steps.

833
01:04:45,330 --> 01:04:50,790
And then maybe you have a
last line to say that part 1--

834
01:04:50,790 --> 01:05:06,670
part 1 and part 2 together show
that alpha squared is equal

835
01:05:06,670 --> 01:05:09,210
to 2, completing the proof.

836
01:05:17,630 --> 01:05:20,390
And again, the point here
was that-- so we already

837
01:05:20,390 --> 01:05:21,890
knew how to prove this.

838
01:05:21,890 --> 01:05:25,396
But how do you write
down a formal proof?

839
01:05:25,396 --> 01:05:30,850
And so, for this proof, it was
divided into these two steps.

840
01:05:30,850 --> 01:05:34,430
So you try to write
each step separately.

841
01:05:34,430 --> 01:05:36,910
They were both--
both of the two steps

842
01:05:36,910 --> 01:05:39,790
was proven by contradiction.

843
01:05:39,790 --> 01:05:41,830
And so you would
say up front-- you

844
01:05:41,830 --> 01:05:45,310
would say that you're going
to prove it by contradiction.

845
01:05:45,310 --> 01:05:48,870
And then, of course,
there would be--

846
01:05:48,870 --> 01:05:50,730
the statement that
you want to prove--

847
01:05:50,730 --> 01:05:53,090
since you're proving
it by contradiction,

848
01:05:53,090 --> 01:05:56,030
you would assume the negation.

849
01:05:56,030 --> 01:05:59,930
And then I think, if you can,
and it doesn't feel too much,

850
01:05:59,930 --> 01:06:03,925
I think it's very helpful to
write what you are trying,

851
01:06:03,925 --> 01:06:06,050
how you are trying to get
the contradiction, right?

852
01:06:06,050 --> 01:06:07,120
That's what I tried here.

853
01:06:09,650 --> 01:06:11,900
And then, you see,
then the big difference

854
01:06:11,900 --> 01:06:14,620
from when we were trying to
figure out how to prove it

855
01:06:14,620 --> 01:06:19,820
is that you now go ahead,
and you just define--

856
01:06:19,820 --> 01:06:21,220
you define those things.

857
01:06:21,220 --> 01:06:24,440
You define h and alpha 2, right?

858
01:06:24,440 --> 01:06:29,460
That seems to come
out of nowhere.

859
01:06:29,460 --> 01:06:32,780
And then you
establish the property

860
01:06:32,780 --> 01:06:35,740
that you want to prove that
leads to the contradiction.

861
01:06:35,740 --> 01:06:39,500
That's the structure.

862
01:06:39,500 --> 01:06:41,330
Are there any questions to this?

863
01:06:43,800 --> 01:06:44,300
Yeah?

864
01:06:44,300 --> 01:06:50,670
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] property
of the real numbers [INAUDIBLE]

865
01:06:50,670 --> 01:06:52,500
least upper bound [INAUDIBLE]?

866
01:06:52,500 --> 01:06:53,417
TOBIAS COLDING: Right.

867
01:06:53,417 --> 01:06:55,340
So we're using a few things.

868
01:06:55,340 --> 01:07:01,580
We're using-- and we're
using a few things.

869
01:07:01,580 --> 01:07:05,100
We're using that it's ordered.

870
01:07:05,100 --> 01:07:09,900
We're also using something about
that it's an ordered field.

871
01:07:09,900 --> 01:07:10,608
And why?

872
01:07:10,608 --> 01:07:12,150
Because we're using
something-- well,

873
01:07:12,150 --> 01:07:14,260
we're using something
about multiplication

874
01:07:14,260 --> 01:07:16,360
to at all define square root.

875
01:07:16,360 --> 01:07:21,220
And also, we're using something
about that 1 is bigger than 0.

876
01:07:30,940 --> 01:07:33,940
And then-- so we're using--
and then we're also using,

877
01:07:33,940 --> 01:07:37,100
of course, completeness in
order to define this least

878
01:07:37,100 --> 01:07:38,140
upper bound.

879
01:07:38,140 --> 01:07:41,420
So we're using a
bunch of these things.

880
01:07:41,420 --> 01:07:44,740
But of course, if you have--

881
01:07:44,740 --> 01:07:47,100
yeah.

882
01:07:47,100 --> 01:07:49,860
I mean, if you're asking when--

883
01:07:49,860 --> 01:07:52,900
and that's not really--

884
01:07:52,900 --> 01:07:59,020
at this stage, very, very soon,
in one of the next few lectures,

885
01:07:59,020 --> 01:08:01,700
things will be in
the real numbers.

886
01:08:01,700 --> 01:08:04,020
We're not going to
work on general fields.

887
01:08:04,020 --> 01:08:06,020
And we're not going to-- yeah.

888
01:08:06,020 --> 01:08:08,500
General fields-- this is
not part of this topic.

889
01:08:08,500 --> 01:08:13,220
But of course, it's
interesting to ask.

890
01:08:13,220 --> 01:08:15,100
And I think that's
your point, is

891
01:08:15,100 --> 01:08:17,340
like, when can you
define something

892
01:08:17,340 --> 01:08:20,260
like a square root
of 2 in a field?

893
01:08:20,260 --> 01:08:21,340
Or something like that.

894
01:08:21,340 --> 01:08:22,600
And there's a lot of things.

895
01:08:22,600 --> 01:08:24,100
You're absolutely
right that there's

896
01:08:24,100 --> 01:08:27,979
a lot of these things that
was just general property.

897
01:08:27,979 --> 01:08:30,279
But maybe we shouldn't
get too much into that.

898
01:08:33,340 --> 01:08:35,620
So I wanted to--

899
01:08:35,620 --> 01:08:39,100
there's a few more things
I was hoping to do.

900
01:08:39,100 --> 01:08:44,859
So I also wanted to just talk
about the Archimedean property

901
01:08:44,859 --> 01:08:45,859
again.

902
01:08:45,859 --> 01:08:56,399
And so I wanted to just outline
how to write a formal proof.

903
01:08:56,399 --> 01:09:01,620
And the reason, of course,
why I'm illustrating how to

904
01:09:01,620 --> 01:09:03,620
write a formal--

905
01:09:03,620 --> 01:09:07,170
writing a formal proof is an
important part of this class.

906
01:09:07,170 --> 01:09:12,010
And I'm obviously
using the things

907
01:09:12,010 --> 01:09:15,490
that we already proved
last time, just so

908
01:09:15,490 --> 01:09:17,676
that you can focus on how--

909
01:09:17,676 --> 01:09:20,729
not to prove-- not
the statements,

910
01:09:20,729 --> 01:09:25,689
why the statements should be
true, but just how to write it.

911
01:09:25,689 --> 01:09:31,660
And so let's talk about
the Archimedean property.

912
01:09:37,850 --> 01:09:41,770
And so the Archimedean
property, just saying that--

913
01:09:41,770 --> 01:09:49,010
so this is the theorem that
the natural numbers is not

914
01:09:49,010 --> 01:09:50,019
bounded from above.

915
01:09:59,050 --> 01:10:01,090
That's one way of
formulating it.

916
01:10:01,090 --> 01:10:03,670
You could also say,
like we did last time,

917
01:10:03,670 --> 01:10:05,970
that whenever you
take a real, there's

918
01:10:05,970 --> 01:10:10,050
a larger natural
number than that real.

919
01:10:10,050 --> 01:10:12,950
But that's the same.

920
01:10:12,950 --> 01:10:13,450
OK.

921
01:10:13,450 --> 01:10:15,310
So now, how do we prove this?

922
01:10:19,370 --> 01:10:21,450
So again, here, we're
thinking about things

923
01:10:21,450 --> 01:10:24,850
as being subset
of R. We always--

924
01:10:24,850 --> 01:10:28,370
when we don't really
mention it, then we always

925
01:10:28,370 --> 01:10:35,530
think about it as being subset
of R. And so we will prove--

926
01:10:35,530 --> 01:10:37,430
we will show this
by contradiction--

927
01:10:44,370 --> 01:10:45,510
by contradiction.

928
01:10:49,210 --> 01:11:02,920
So suppose alpha is an upper
bound for the natural numbers.

929
01:11:05,730 --> 01:11:22,290
Well, we can assume, after
replacing alpha by sup of A,

930
01:11:22,290 --> 01:11:33,900
we can assume that alpha
is the least upper bound.

931
01:11:43,650 --> 01:11:48,730
And now-- and then I
think it's nice, again,

932
01:11:48,730 --> 01:11:54,860
to write where the
contradiction is coming from.

933
01:12:02,010 --> 01:12:33,890
So we'll show-- we will get the
desired contradiction by showing

934
01:12:33,890 --> 01:12:50,370
that alpha minus 1 is also
an upper bound, contradicting

935
01:12:50,370 --> 01:12:53,910
that alpha was the
least upper bound.

936
01:13:03,860 --> 01:13:11,180
And so now, since alpha--

937
01:13:11,180 --> 01:13:14,440
so this is what
we want to prove.

938
01:13:14,440 --> 01:13:32,880
Since alpha is an upper bound
for all natural numbers,

939
01:13:32,880 --> 01:13:35,000
if you add 1 to
the natural number,

940
01:13:35,000 --> 01:13:39,200
this is also-- this here is
also a natural number, right?

941
01:13:39,200 --> 01:13:41,300
n plus 1 is also
a natural number.

942
01:13:41,300 --> 01:13:46,820
So n plus 1 must be less or
equal to this upper bound.

943
01:13:50,000 --> 01:14:01,120
But this implies that n--

944
01:14:01,120 --> 01:14:05,280
subtracting 1 on both sides
implies that n is less or equal

945
01:14:05,280 --> 01:14:09,120
to alpha minus 1, and this--

946
01:14:09,120 --> 01:14:20,260
which holds for all n,
all natural numbers.

947
01:14:20,260 --> 01:14:26,620
So alpha minus 1 is
also an upper bound.

948
01:14:40,960 --> 01:14:41,640
OK.

949
01:14:41,640 --> 01:14:48,720
And this proof gives you
the desired contradiction.

950
01:14:48,720 --> 01:14:52,720
So that's how you
would write that.

951
01:14:52,720 --> 01:14:57,360
Let me talk a little
bit about sequences.

952
01:14:57,360 --> 01:15:00,440
So this will be a big topic.

953
01:15:00,440 --> 01:15:01,660
So sequences.

954
01:15:06,600 --> 01:15:09,180
Well, first-- so we talked
about that a little bit.

955
01:15:09,180 --> 01:15:22,640
So if you look at the-- so how
do we think about square root

956
01:15:22,640 --> 01:15:26,340
2 on the real line?

957
01:15:26,340 --> 01:15:30,040
So the way to think about square
root of 2 is what we did here--

958
01:15:30,040 --> 01:15:34,340
that is, this positive thing
so that the square is 2.

959
01:15:34,340 --> 01:15:39,960
But if you want to place square
root of 2 on the real line,

960
01:15:39,960 --> 01:15:46,060
then you could think about it
as 1 as a limit of a sequence.

961
01:15:46,060 --> 01:15:51,280
If you take 1, and then
the 1 is less than 1.4,

962
01:15:51,280 --> 01:16:02,990
which is less than 1.41, which
is less than 1.414, et cetera.

963
01:16:02,990 --> 01:16:07,590
And so this here is a sequence.

964
01:16:07,590 --> 01:16:10,050
I'll make that precise
in just a minute,

965
01:16:10,050 --> 01:16:13,110
but an increasing
sequence of numbers.

966
01:16:13,110 --> 01:16:17,210
And the limit is
square root of 2.

967
01:16:17,210 --> 01:16:21,470
So if you plug square root
of 2 in on the calculator,

968
01:16:21,470 --> 01:16:23,370
it will bring out
a lot of digits.

969
01:16:23,370 --> 01:16:24,970
And these are the first digits.

970
01:16:24,970 --> 01:16:27,070
And it will continue on.

971
01:16:27,070 --> 01:16:30,270
And that's how you think
about square root of 2

972
01:16:30,270 --> 01:16:34,670
if you want to think
about it on the real line.

973
01:16:34,670 --> 01:16:36,750
And so we want to--

974
01:16:36,750 --> 01:16:40,870
and so square root of 2 is
going to be the limit of these.

975
01:16:40,870 --> 01:16:42,570
And we need to
make this precise.

976
01:16:42,570 --> 01:16:48,990
And we also need to make precise
what, really, a sequence is.

977
01:16:48,990 --> 01:17:05,740
So a sequence-- so a
sequence of real numbers--

978
01:17:13,750 --> 01:17:14,720
this is a map.

979
01:17:17,310 --> 01:17:24,630
It's a function from the
natural numbers into R.

980
01:17:24,630 --> 01:17:29,910
So for each natural number,
you get a real number.

981
01:17:29,910 --> 01:17:35,150
And so a function--

982
01:17:35,150 --> 01:17:39,990
a function is defined by the
value at different points.

983
01:17:39,990 --> 01:17:42,690
So this thing here--

984
01:17:42,690 --> 01:17:46,610
but if you want to think
about it as a sequence,

985
01:17:46,610 --> 01:17:51,870
then it's common to denote it--
to denote this here like that.

986
01:17:51,870 --> 01:17:55,430
So this is how one typically
writes a sequence, where,

987
01:17:55,430 --> 01:18:03,570
instead of what the argument is,
what this is, inside a bracket,

988
01:18:03,570 --> 01:18:07,000
you write it as a subscript.

989
01:18:07,000 --> 01:18:10,150
So this is a sequence.

990
01:18:10,150 --> 01:18:15,550
And so, in the case
over there, if you're

991
01:18:15,550 --> 01:18:20,470
thinking about a1,
say, to be equal to 1,

992
01:18:20,470 --> 01:18:25,830
a2 to be equal to 1.4, a3--

993
01:18:25,830 --> 01:18:28,830
and now I'm thinking
about the sequence here.

994
01:18:28,830 --> 01:18:31,790
As I'm thinking about
square root of 2,

995
01:18:31,790 --> 01:18:36,470
and I take the number
of the subscript here,

996
01:18:36,470 --> 01:18:39,870
I'm just taking the number of
digits in square root of 2.

997
01:18:39,870 --> 01:18:41,630
So now I need three digits.

998
01:18:41,630 --> 01:18:46,310
Here, I need a 2, 1, 4, 1.

999
01:18:46,310 --> 01:18:53,920
a4 is 1.414, et cetera.

1000
01:18:53,920 --> 01:18:58,540
And so then you have--
so then a n here

1001
01:18:58,540 --> 01:19:05,780
is a sequence of real numbers.

1002
01:19:05,780 --> 01:19:07,700
And it's actually increasing--

1003
01:19:07,700 --> 01:19:12,300
that the sequence a n
is less than a n plus 1.

1004
01:19:12,300 --> 01:19:14,540
We have this property.

1005
01:19:14,540 --> 01:19:20,620
And we want to talk about-- next
time, we want to talk about,

1006
01:19:20,620 --> 01:19:23,100
what does a limit mean?

1007
01:19:23,100 --> 01:19:24,670
What is a limit?

1008
01:19:27,380 --> 01:19:31,780
And so square root
of 2, indeed, will

1009
01:19:31,780 --> 01:19:33,880
be the limit of this sequence.

1010
01:19:33,880 --> 01:19:36,500
And the limit, you write
like this, sometimes,

1011
01:19:36,500 --> 01:19:40,940
as n goes to infinity of a n.

1012
01:19:40,940 --> 01:19:43,810
But we'll talk about
this next time.

